              {
                "text": "**Analyzing Pixel Transformations**\n\nI'm focusing on the 8x8 grid, with a background of 7. Observing the impact on corner pixels is key. Training data reveals how initial values, like 1 at (0,0) and 4 at (7,7), transform in the output. The input value of 1 becomes a 2x2 block in the output, while 4 maps to a different location.\n\n\n**Uncovering Grid Patterns**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on the output patterns, specifically examining how the input values transform based on their original corner positions within the 8x8 grid. I've observed that values at (0,0) and (0,7) result in 2x2 blocks at predictable output locations. Interestingly, the behavior of the bottom-right corner seems different. I'm also puzzled by the fact that some values don't appear to transform at all.\n\n\n**Discovering Output Symmetries**\n\nI've made a breakthrough. Analyzing bottom-left versus bottom-right corner transformations, I've noticed a mirror symmetry across the grid's center line. It's like a diagonal or a corner-shaped \"hook\" pattern. Top corners are 2x2 squares, but bottom ones create this 3-pixel shape. This deviation from the top corners has me completely stumped.\n\n\n",
                "type": "reasoning_text"
              }
